{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 asserts that the flags are sustained NOT because the two critics independently found the same counterexample, but because independent NU verdicts (SRC_006 and SRC_008) on a prior artifact established a meta-judgment layer confirming the critics' verdicts as sound. This introduces a meta-layer confirmation mechanism that the problem standard does not invoke. The §11.3 ritual adjudication standard concerns adjudicating the critic's verdicts directly — the problem says 'audit the critic,' not 'audit the auditors of the critic.' By shifting the basis of sustained flags to a peer-review meta-layer rather than the critics' own findings, SRC_001 substitutes an unjustified rationale for the one the standard actually provides, mischaracterizing why the flags are sustained.","counterexample":["Under the same §11.3 standard, if SRC_006 and SRC_008 had not existed or had disagreed, but the two critics still independently found the same valid counterexample, the flags would still be sustained on the strength of those counterexamples alone — demonstrating that the meta-layer is not the operative basis the standard relies on."],"premise":"The problem standard (§11.3) sustains adjudication-ritual flags based on the critics' verdicts themselves, not on a meta-judgment peer-review layer external to those verdicts.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"000000000000","quote":"audit the critic: adjudication-ritual flags sustained (§11.3)"}],"target":"579b3c9627658dbb4570cc4f2c5abf947fbc586dba59632b55900597f87d7377"}]}